1
00:00:01,064 --> 00:00:01,530
Riffle.

2
00:00:01,580 --> 00:00:05,507
An explainable AI co-pilot for citizen stream science.

3
00:00:05,557 --> 00:00:10,146
Built for the IEEE OneAquaHealth Global Hackathon, track three:

4
00:00:10,196 --> 00:00:11,914
AI-supported assessment.

5
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:15,358
Urban streams can turn dangerous within hours,

6
00:00:15,408 --> 00:00:18,159
after a sewer overflow or a summer algal bloom.

7
00:00:18,209 --> 00:00:20,959
Yet they are monitored only a few times a year.

8
00:00:21,009 --> 00:00:24,356
Riffle lets any neighbour check a stream in five minutes,

9
00:00:24,406 --> 00:00:28,527
with an AI co-pilot that double-checks with them, not instead of them.

10
00:00:28,577 --> 00:00:32,877
And it turns every observation into One Health insight and early warning.

11
00:00:33,628 --> 00:00:37,145
The home screen answers the question people actually have:

12
00:00:37,195 --> 00:00:38,497
is my stream OK today?

13
00:00:38,547 --> 00:00:43,294
At the Vale das Flores park outlet, the latest check found no animals in warm,

14
00:00:43,344 --> 00:00:46,738
green water, so Riffle advises keeping dogs on the lead.

15
00:00:46,788 --> 00:00:49,382
The three rings are the One Health reading:

16
00:00:49,432 --> 00:00:51,288
ecosystem, people, and animals.

17
00:00:52,038 --> 00:00:53,081
Let's do a check.

18
00:00:53,131 --> 00:00:56,811
Everything is plain language and pictures, with no jargon.

19
00:00:56,861 --> 00:01:00,284
The water here is murky and grey, with a sewage smell.

20
00:01:00,334 --> 00:01:02,278
Then a quick look at the banks:

21
00:01:02,328 --> 00:01:06,458
plants, shade, litter, and any pipes discharging into the stream.

22
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:08,202
Now the bug hunt.

23
00:01:08,252 --> 00:01:11,023
Riffle uses the miniSASS biomonitoring method.

24
00:01:11,073 --> 00:01:15,927
Every animal group has a how-to-tell guide with its key identification features,

25
00:01:15,977 --> 00:01:19,054
and there is a built-in five-minute sampling timer.

26
00:01:19,104 --> 00:01:23,959
I'll record stoneflies and flat mayflies, which are very sensitive to pollution,

27
00:01:24,009 --> 00:01:24,940
plus some worms.

28
00:01:25,691 --> 00:01:27,338
This is the heart of Riffle.

29
00:01:27,388 --> 00:01:32,368
Only now, after I've recorded my animals, does the co-pilot reveal its expectation,

30
00:01:32,418 --> 00:01:34,064
so it can't bias what I see.

31
00:01:34,114 --> 00:01:38,973
A transparent model predicts the score a stream that looks like this should have,

32
00:01:39,023 --> 00:01:41,761
and shows exactly which observations drove it.

33
00:01:41,811 --> 00:01:43,215
It raises two questions:

34
00:01:43,265 --> 00:01:46,427
an expert rule says stoneflies rarely survive sewage,

35
00:01:46,477 --> 00:01:49,881
and the model says my animals score higher than expected.

36
00:01:50,632 --> 00:01:51,962
I'm the scientist here.

37
00:01:52,012 --> 00:01:54,842
I can go back and check, or confirm with a note.

38
00:01:54,892 --> 00:01:56,882
I counted two tails, so I confirm.

39
00:01:56,932 --> 00:02:01,202
Because the question stays open, the record is published as preliminary,

40
00:02:01,252 --> 00:02:02,822
and sent to a local expert.

41
00:02:03,572 --> 00:02:06,669
I get a One Health reading with practical advice,

42
00:02:06,719 --> 00:02:10,651
experience points that reward careful science, and new badges.

43
00:02:10,701 --> 00:02:13,798
And the same check becomes an HL7 FHIR R4 bundle:

44
00:02:13,848 --> 00:02:17,266
observations, the co-pilot's flags as detected issues,

45
00:02:17,316 --> 00:02:22,596
and provenance recording what the citizen, the AI, and the expert each contributed.

46
00:02:22,646 --> 00:02:23,752
It validates live.

47
00:02:24,504 --> 00:02:30,005
In the expert review queue, experts see the citizen's note next to the model's evidence,

48
00:02:30,055 --> 00:02:32,023
and verify or reject in seconds.

49
00:02:32,073 --> 00:02:36,186
Riffle also tracks how often flagged records turn out to be valid.

50
00:02:36,236 --> 00:02:38,394
That is how we audit the AI itself.

51
00:02:39,145 --> 00:02:43,678
For cities, the map shows stream health, or seven-day risk, across Coimbra,

52
00:02:43,728 --> 00:02:46,427
and ranks what is stressing the streams most:

53
00:02:46,477 --> 00:02:48,505
a to-do list for the municipality.

54
00:02:49,256 --> 00:02:51,790
On the urban reach of the Coselhas stream,

55
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:55,604
the trend shows the decline since construction began upstream.

56
00:02:55,654 --> 00:03:00,217
This presence map shows sensitive insects disappearing, quarter by quarter:

57
00:03:00,267 --> 00:03:02,186
an early warning sign in itself.

58
00:03:02,938 --> 00:03:08,106
Early warning combines the live Open-Meteo forecast with each site's vulnerability and

59
00:03:08,156 --> 00:03:12,960
the latest citizen observations, covering sewer overflow, heat and algal blooms,

60
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:13,991
and flash floods.

61
00:03:14,041 --> 00:03:16,600
Here is the storm and heatwave stress test,

62
00:03:16,650 --> 00:03:20,908
with the affected sites and concrete actions for people and pet owners.

63
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:27,032
To build awareness, a storytelling module follows one raindrop from city rooftops to a

64
00:03:27,082 --> 00:03:31,760
poisoned dog, and a glossary translates scientific jargon into plain words.

65
00:03:32,511 --> 00:03:34,676
Finally, open data and standards:

66
00:03:34,726 --> 00:03:37,562
bulk FHIR and CSV export, the architecture,

67
00:03:37,612 --> 00:03:43,134
and a model card that documents exactly how the AI works, and where its limits are.

68
00:03:43,184 --> 00:03:47,631
Everything runs on the device, works offline, and needs no account.

69
00:03:48,382 --> 00:03:48,957
Riffle.

70
00:03:49,007 --> 00:03:51,100
From streams to systems.

71
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:56,012
Try it now at karan6705 dot github dot io slash riffle.
